# Deterministic proof of the Events.cpp:195/201/205 ordering defect.
# Captured 2026-08-31T21:35:55Z on Linux 6.8.0-124-generic, libX11 
# Sources committed beside this file. Rebuild: gcc -o p wedge-probe.c -lX11

=== wedge-probe.c (replays wm2 nextEvent lines 195/201/205) ===
wm2 line 195  -> QLength=0  (0 means the branch is NOT taken)
              -> fd readable=1
wm2 line 201  -> XFlush() called
wm2 line 205  -> QLength=1, fd readable=0

*** WEDGE CONFIRMED ***
XFlush() moved the event into Xlib's queue and drained the socket.
wm2 does not re-test QLength after line 201, so poll() at line 205
blocks indefinitely on an event the process is already holding.
exit=1

=== wedge-probe-both-shapes.c (old shape vs proposed fix) ===
OLD (wm2 today):
  line195 saw QLength=0, fell through to XFlush
  line205 poll() -> TIMED OUT: would block forever with timeout=-1 (QLength is now 1)

NEW (proposed fix):
  XPending()=1 -> event delivered immediately (type=28)
  poll() never reached; no wedge possible
